\title{dmlrs: fast double / debiased machine learning}
\description{
Double / debiased machine learning (Chernozhukov et al., 2018) with the
estimation, including the nuisance learners, implemented in Rust. The
models follow the DoubleML package: \code{\link[=dml_plr]{dml_plr()}}, \code{\link[=dml_irm]{dml_irm()}}, \code{\link[=dml_pliv]{dml_pliv()}},
\code{\link[=dml_iivm]{dml_iivm()}}, \code{\link[=dml_did]{dml_did()}}, \code{\link[=dml_staggered_did]{dml_staggered_did()}}, \code{\link[=dml_rdflex]{dml_rdflex()}} and
\code{\link[=dml_apos]{dml_apos()}}. Nuisance learners are created
with the \link{learners} constructors such as \code{\link[=lrn_lasso_cv]{lrn_lasso_cv()}} or
\code{\link[=lrn_random_forest]{lrn_random_forest()}}.
}
\details{
Heterogeneous effects: the best linear predictor of the CATE and group
effects of a fit (\code{\link[=cate]{cate()}}, \code{\link[=gate]{gate()}}), CATE meta-learners
(\code{\link[=cate_learner]{cate_learner()}}), causal forests (\code{\link[=causal_forest]{causal_forest()}}, a port of \code{grf}) and
conformal intervals for individual effects (\code{\link[=conformal_ite]{conformal_ite()}}).

Beyond DoubleML: dose-response curves (\code{\link[=dml_dose_response]{dml_dose_response()}}), causal
mediation (\code{\link[=dml_mediation]{dml_mediation()}}), difference in
differences with a continuous treatment (\code{\link[=dml_contdid]{dml_contdid()}}), effects on a
censored time to event (\code{\link[=dml_survival]{dml_survival()}}), synthetic
difference in differences (\code{\link[=synth_did]{synth_did()}}, a port of \code{synthdid}), optimal
policy trees (\code{\link[=policy_tree]{policy_tree()}}, a port of \code{policytree}) with
\code{\link[=policy_value]{policy_value()}}, and Lee bounds under sample selection (\code{\link[=lee_bounds]{lee_bounds()}}).

The same Rust library powers the Python package dml-rs, whose results are
validated against DoubleML; see \url{https://asadgudzevic94.github.io/dml-rs/}.
The R reference and articles are at \url{https://asadgudzevic94.github.io/dml-rs/r/}.
}
\section{Threads}{

Cross-fitting and the forest and boosting learners run in parallel on all
cores. To use fewer, set \code{options(dmlrs.threads = n)} or the environment
variable \code{RAYON_NUM_THREADS} before the package is loaded. Under
\verb{R CMD check} with \verb{_R_CHECK_LIMIT_CORES_} set (as on CRAN), the package
uses two threads.
}

